    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_034e93435b32d5306e3f3bc6b1e53193e80215837bdbcd24f0ff9e0207fde23b",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Source-grounded mathematics at mechanism level: averaging quadratic characters over fundamental discriminants vanishes away from square support and yields local corrections on square support; restricting discriminants to a progression introduces gcd, residue-class, and power-of-two conditions analyzed through a counting Dirichlet series, its pole at one, and Chinese-remainder splitting. Conceptual interpretation: the quadratic-family analogue of a diagonal is square-class membership rather than equality of two products. The exceptional factor at two comes from the fact that, when the square-part parameter is even, only some coprime residue classes contain fundamental discriminants; odd and even cases therefore require separate counting. Corrupted square glyphs and h₂ displays make all exact formulas and constants scan-dependent.",
