    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_a47261d1a8e7b50d1ea7d014a6bd373a8ff77dd800834c10e599bf26f29912ff",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Source-grounded mathematics: approximate functional equations followed by family averaging act as harmonic detectors; diagonal terms can control low moments, whereas progressively subtler off-diagonal contributions enter higher moments. The authors' recipe nevertheless uses only diagonal data and conjectures the simple answer left after unexplained cancellations. Conceptual interpretation: this is answer-level compression rather than a derivation—the recipe organizes a predicted post-cancellation form but neither explains nor bounds the off-diagonal pieces it needs. Families with different detectors can still exhibit the same symmetry-class answer, which the source marks as an unresolved mechanism.",
