    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_f9759c187e59a681ea3b2aab8b9696ad128124bf5d16a5bea4303547d0f140e9",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Source-grounded mathematics: the paper defines an operational character family whose Dirichlet series are primitive L-functions, orders it by log conductor, requires shared or monotone functional-equation data, and assumes multiplicative limits for expected coefficient products. Conceptual interpretation: rather than classify families by mathematical origin, this recipe-specific definition retains exactly the conductor evolution and multiplicative orthogonality needed to turn averaged multivariable Dirichlet series into Euler products. Existence of the limits and counting asymptotics is assumed, and the definition is deliberately restrictive rather than a general ontology of L-function families.",
